# Scripts that read the bytes of a rendered page without going through the
# shared reader in tests/guard-paths.sh, and why each is entitled to.
#
# Held by tests/check-raster-reading.sh. Every other script that takes bytes
# out of a page must call guard_pnm_pixels or guard_pnm_ink: finding where a
# raster starts is a grammar with a comment rule and a per-format token
# count, and getting it wrong does not fail -- it shifts every pixel and
# yields a plausible number -- so it is written once.
#
# An entry naming a script that is not there is itself a failure, so that an
# exemption cannot outlive what it exempted and quietly cover a later script
# of the same name.
#
#   script  reason

run-raster-formats-test.sh  the header is its subject rather than something in its way: it holds every raster device's magic number, dimensions and maximum against the bytes they must be, so a reader that stepped over the header would step over the whole test

run-pdf-consumer-test.sh  it compares pages from two different producers, whose headers agree on nothing but length, so it takes the raster as the last width times height bytes -- arithmetic that holds for either -- rather than parsing a grammar only one of them is bound by

run-region-replay-test.sh  it compares regions between two renders of the same page, so it wants ranges of bytes rather than the values of pixels, and it finds the raster the same way -- the last width times height bytes -- because the two renders it compares need not have written the same header
